1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,794
Three hundred and fifty metres
below the surface,

2
00:00:05,814 --> 00:00:10,273
a drilling riser connects a floating rig to a
wellhead only a few metres tall.

3
00:00:10,893 --> 00:00:14,851
Every wave, every current and every metre
of rig offset ends up here,

4
00:00:14,871 --> 00:00:18,657
as bending in the wellhead, the
conductor, and the soil around it.

5
00:00:19,377 --> 00:00:22,224
To know whether this well will
last its design life,

6
00:00:22,244 --> 00:00:25,919
we need a finite element model, and we
need to be able to trust it.

7
00:00:26,639 --> 00:00:29,400
In this video we build that
model step by step:

8
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:32,715
which element types to use, how
fine the mesh has to be,

9
00:00:32,735 --> 00:00:35,969
and which boundary conditions
belong at each interface.

10
00:00:35,989 --> 00:00:39,461
And we'll see what goes wrong when
those choices are wrong.

11
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:44,350
First, the structure.

12
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:46,584
This is a four-slot subsea template:

13
00:00:46,604 --> 00:00:50,494
a steel frame on the seabed, held down
by four suction anchors,

14
00:00:50,514 --> 00:00:53,846
with a protective roof that
fishing trawls slide over.

15
00:00:54,466 --> 00:00:56,079
Each slot holds one well.

16
00:00:56,099 --> 00:00:58,824
The first part installed is the conductor:

17
00:00:58,844 --> 00:01:03,726
a 36-inch steel pipe, jetted or drilled
about sixty metres into the seabed.

18
00:01:04,246 --> 00:01:07,584
The conductor housing is welded to
the top of the conductor.

19
00:01:07,604 --> 00:01:09,992
It's also called the
low-pressure wellhead,

20
00:01:10,012 --> 00:01:12,847
and it sits in the template's
well-slot receptacle.

21
00:01:13,368 --> 00:01:16,226
The surface casing runs
inside the conductor.

22
00:01:16,246 --> 00:01:18,720
It's a 20-inch pipe, cemented in place.

23
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:23,661
The high-pressure wellhead housing is welded
to the top of the surface casing.

24
00:01:23,681 --> 00:01:26,622
It lands on a shoulder inside the
conductor housing,

25
00:01:26,642 --> 00:01:29,583
and a lock ring preloads it down
onto that shoulder.

26
00:01:30,103 --> 00:01:34,207
Then the blowout preventer lands on top, and
the riser connects it to the rig.

27
00:01:34,827 --> 00:01:37,341
Every load goes down through that stack:

28
00:01:37,361 --> 00:01:42,600
weight, riser tension, and above all, a bending
moment that cycles with every wave.

29
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:46,402
That cycling bending moment is what
drives wellhead fatigue.

30
00:01:48,033 --> 00:01:49,992
Before you choose a single element,

31
00:01:50,012 --> 00:01:52,762
write down the questions the
model has to answer.

32
00:01:52,782 --> 00:01:54,514
The questions decide the model.

33
00:01:55,134 --> 00:01:56,619
Some are global questions.

34
00:01:56,639 --> 00:02:00,901
How is the load shared between the
conductor, the casing and the template?

35
00:02:00,921 --> 00:02:03,620
How far down into the soil
does the bending go?

36
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:06,176
For those we build a global model,

37
00:02:06,196 --> 00:02:09,200
with beams for the pipes and
springs for the soil.

38
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:12,646
It runs in seconds, which makes
sensitivity studies easy.

39
00:02:13,265 --> 00:02:14,817
Others are local questions.

40
00:02:14,837 --> 00:02:16,738
What is the stress at a weld toe?

41
00:02:16,758 --> 00:02:20,464
How is the contact pressure distributed
on the landing shoulder?

42
00:02:20,484 --> 00:02:23,083
A beam model has no
information about either.

43
00:02:23,603 --> 00:02:27,315
For those we cut out the wellhead,
build a local solid model,

44
00:02:27,335 --> 00:02:30,986
and drive its boundaries with results
from the global model.

45
00:02:31,006 --> 00:02:34,290
This is called global-local
analysis, or submodelling.

46
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:38,319
Chapter one:

47
00:02:38,339 --> 00:02:39,703
choosing the element type.

48
00:02:39,723 --> 00:02:43,854
There are four families, and each one makes an
assumption about the structure.

49
00:02:44,474 --> 00:02:48,694
A beam element reduces a member to a line
with a cross-section attached.

50
00:02:48,714 --> 00:02:53,111
It assumes the member is slender, roughly
ten times longer than it is deep,

51
00:02:53,131 --> 00:02:57,116
and it gives you section forces and
deflections, not local stresses.

52
00:02:57,136 --> 00:03:00,885
The conductor is more than sixty times
longer than its diameter,

53
00:03:00,905 --> 00:03:02,239
so it's a natural beam.

54
00:03:02,859 --> 00:03:05,984
A shell element reduces a thin
wall to its mid-surface.

55
00:03:06,004 --> 00:03:09,929
It assumes the thickness is small
compared with the other dimensions,

56
00:03:09,949 --> 00:03:11,930
like the skirt of a suction anchor,

57
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:15,418
where the radius is about a hundred
times the wall thickness.

58
00:03:16,038 --> 00:03:19,237
A solid element makes no
geometric assumption at all.

59
00:03:19,257 --> 00:03:23,307
That makes it the most general family,
and also the most expensive.

60
00:03:23,327 --> 00:03:27,437
You need solids for thick, complex parts
like the wellhead housings,

61
00:03:27,457 --> 00:03:32,539
where the wall is a third of the radius, and
every groove and shoulder carries load.

62
00:03:33,159 --> 00:03:37,070
An axisymmetric element is a shortcut
for solids of revolution.

63
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:41,874
If the geometry, the loads and the supports
are all symmetric about one axis,

64
00:03:41,894 --> 00:03:46,242
a two-dimensional section gives you the
full three-dimensional answer.

65
00:03:47,867 --> 00:03:51,983
Solid elements come in two main shapes,
tetrahedra and hexahedra,

66
00:03:52,003 --> 00:03:54,529
and in two orders, linear and quadratic.

67
00:03:54,549 --> 00:03:57,648
This choice matters more than
most people expect.

68
00:03:58,268 --> 00:03:59,455
Here's a simple test:

69
00:03:59,475 --> 00:04:01,524
a cantilever with a load at its tip,

70
00:04:01,544 --> 00:04:04,455
meshed with only one element
through the thickness.

71
00:04:04,475 --> 00:04:07,214
We know the correct deflection
from beam theory.

72
00:04:07,733 --> 00:04:12,708
The linear hexahedron reaches only sixty-five
percent of the correct deflection.

73
00:04:12,728 --> 00:04:15,830
The linear tetrahedron reaches
twenty-two percent.

74
00:04:15,850 --> 00:04:17,266
Both are far too stiff.

75
00:04:17,286 --> 00:04:18,702
This is called locking.

76
00:04:19,322 --> 00:04:19,969
Here's why.

77
00:04:19,989 --> 00:04:23,550
In bending, the material between two
cross-sections curves.

78
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:25,735
A linear element has straight edges,

79
00:04:25,755 --> 00:04:29,012
so the only way it can follow the
bending is to shear.

80
00:04:29,032 --> 00:04:33,685
That shear doesn't exist in the real beam,
but it still stores strain energy,

81
00:04:33,705 --> 00:04:35,627
so the element is far too stiff.

82
00:04:35,647 --> 00:04:36,962
This is shear locking.

83
00:04:37,582 --> 00:04:39,656
A common fix is reduced integration,

84
00:04:39,676 --> 00:04:43,845
which samples the strain at a single point
in the middle of the element.

85
00:04:43,865 --> 00:04:47,568
But this hourglass-shaped mode produces
no strain at that point,

86
00:04:47,588 --> 00:04:48,848
so nothing resists it.

87
00:04:48,868 --> 00:04:53,677
Without hourglass control, our cantilever with
one element through the thickness is

88
00:04:53,697 --> 00:04:57,459
singular, and with two, the deflection
comes out twice too large.

89
00:04:58,079 --> 00:05:02,105
Quadratic elements have mid-side nodes,
so their edges can curve.

90
00:05:02,125 --> 00:05:04,533
With one element through the thickness,

91
00:05:04,553 --> 00:05:09,264
the twenty-node hexahedron and the ten-node
tetrahedron both land within two

92
00:05:09,284 --> 00:05:11,132
percent of the correct answer.

93
00:05:11,152 --> 00:05:16,299
Linear hexahedra with incompatible modes, an
enhanced formulation, do just as well.

94
00:05:16,919 --> 00:05:17,736
So, the rules.

95
00:05:17,756 --> 00:05:21,746
Use quadratic tetrahedra when the
geometry is too complex to sweep.

96
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:24,917
Use hexahedra wherever you can
sweep or map the mesh:

97
00:05:24,937 --> 00:05:27,909
they give the most accuracy per
degree of freedom.

98
00:05:27,929 --> 00:05:30,242
Don't use linear tetrahedra for stress.

99
00:05:30,262 --> 00:05:35,029
And with linear hexahedra, use an enhanced
formulation or at least four elements

100
00:05:35,049 --> 00:05:38,261
through the thickness, and check
the hourglass energy.

101
00:05:39,867 --> 00:05:42,811
The wellhead housings are
solids of revolution,

102
00:05:42,831 --> 00:05:45,333
so let's take the axisymmetric shortcut.

103
00:05:45,353 --> 00:05:50,378
One section of about a thousand quadrilateral
elements solves in a fraction of a

104
00:05:50,398 --> 00:05:50,820
second.

105
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:55,993
Revolve the same section around the axis and
we get a swept hexahedral mesh,

106
00:05:56,013 --> 00:06:00,746
with thirty-seven thousand elements and a
hundred and forty thousand degrees of

107
00:06:00,766 --> 00:06:01,227
freedom.

108
00:06:01,247 --> 00:06:05,921
Under preload alone, the two models agree to
better than a tenth of a percent.

109
00:06:06,541 --> 00:06:08,997
But the bending moment isn't axisymmetric.

110
00:06:09,017 --> 00:06:13,477
It squeezes the landing shoulder on one side
and pulls it open on the other.

111
00:06:13,497 --> 00:06:17,426
Once the shoulder separates, the contact
is different on each side,

112
00:06:17,446 --> 00:06:20,787
and an axisymmetric section simply
cannot represent that.

113
00:06:21,407 --> 00:06:26,246
This is where axisymmetric elements are no
longer enough, and we have to go to 3D.

114
00:06:26,266 --> 00:06:28,143
We can still save half the work.

115
00:06:28,163 --> 00:06:32,943
Bending in one plane is symmetric about that
plane, so we model half the housing,

116
00:06:32,963 --> 00:06:37,803
and put a symmetry condition on the cut face,
with zero displacement normal to it.

117
00:06:38,423 --> 00:06:39,782
That's the general pattern:

118
00:06:39,802 --> 00:06:42,746
start with the cheapest element that
captures the physics,

119
00:06:42,766 --> 00:06:44,841
and know which physics would rule it out.

120
00:06:48,267 --> 00:06:48,932
Chapter two:

121
00:06:48,952 --> 00:06:49,503
mesh size.

122
00:06:49,523 --> 00:06:50,875
The principle is simple.

123
00:06:50,895 --> 00:06:54,131
The mesh has to be fine where the
stress changes quickly,

124
00:06:54,151 --> 00:06:56,302
and it can be coarse where it doesn't.

125
00:06:56,922 --> 00:07:01,300
Take a shoulder with a fillet radius, like
the steps in a wellhead housing,

126
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,294
and refine the mesh at the fillet.

127
00:07:03,314 --> 00:07:07,869
With one element across the radius, the peak
stress is twenty percent too low.

128
00:07:07,889 --> 00:07:12,560
With about twelve quadratic elements around the
fillet, it's within one percent.

129
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:14,789
Refine further, and it hardly changes.

130
00:07:14,809 --> 00:07:16,314
That's a converged result.

131
00:07:16,933 --> 00:07:18,487
Now make the corner sharp.

132
00:07:18,507 --> 00:07:20,667
Each time we halve the element size,

133
00:07:20,687 --> 00:07:25,330
the peak stress rises by about thirty-seven
percent, and it never levels off.

134
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:26,602
That's a singularity.

135
00:07:26,622 --> 00:07:31,326
In linear elastic theory, the stress at a
sharp re-entrant corner is infinite,

136
00:07:31,346 --> 00:07:34,233
and refining the mesh only
shows you more of it.

137
00:07:34,853 --> 00:07:37,667
So never report a peak stress
at a singularity.

138
00:07:37,687 --> 00:07:42,673
Model the real radius, read the stress at a
distance with a method designed for it,

139
00:07:42,693 --> 00:07:44,241
or use fracture mechanics.

140
00:07:44,861 --> 00:07:46,490
Here are some starting rules.

141
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:51,268
Put at least two quadratic elements, or four
linear ones, through a wall in bending.

142
00:07:51,288 --> 00:07:54,852
Use about ten to twelve elements
around a ninety degree fillet.

143
00:07:54,872 --> 00:07:59,288
Keep the size change between neighbouring
elements under about thirty percent.

144
00:07:59,308 --> 00:08:02,701
And where the stress matters, keep
the elements well shaped,

145
00:08:02,721 --> 00:08:05,432
with a low aspect ratio and no
heavy distortion.

146
00:08:06,053 --> 00:08:08,572
But rules of thumb only get you started.

147
00:08:08,592 --> 00:08:12,761
The real test is a convergence study on
the result you care about:

148
00:08:12,781 --> 00:08:14,475
refine, rerun, and compare.

149
00:08:14,495 --> 00:08:18,094
If the result is still changing,
the mesh isn't finished.

150
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:21,485
Welds need their own treatment.

151
00:08:21,505 --> 00:08:25,618
A weld toe is sharp, so the local stress
there doesn't converge either.

152
00:08:26,138 --> 00:08:30,243
For fatigue, standards such as
DNV-RP-C203 use the hot-spot method.

153
00:08:30,263 --> 00:08:33,690
Mesh the wall with elements about
one thickness in size,

154
00:08:33,710 --> 00:08:38,738
read the surface stress at half a thickness and
at one and a half thicknesses from

155
00:08:38,758 --> 00:08:41,817
the toe, and extrapolate
linearly back to the toe.

156
00:08:41,837 --> 00:08:46,126
The S-N curves are calibrated to that
procedure, so follow it exactly.

157
00:08:46,146 --> 00:08:49,081
A finer mesh does not give a
better answer here.

158
00:08:49,702 --> 00:08:51,505
There's a quick mesh check, too.

159
00:08:51,525 --> 00:08:54,410
Compare the averaged and
unaveraged nodal stresses.

160
00:08:54,430 --> 00:08:57,202
Where they differ by more than
about ten percent,

161
00:08:57,222 --> 00:08:59,879
the mesh is too coarse for the
stress gradient.

162
00:09:03,300 --> 00:09:04,141
Chapter three:

163
00:09:04,161 --> 00:09:04,694
the soil.

164
00:09:04,714 --> 00:09:08,382
The conductor gets all of its lateral
support from the soil,

165
00:09:08,402 --> 00:09:11,640
so the soil model controls the
whole global response.

166
00:09:12,260 --> 00:09:15,898
You could mesh the soil as a
continuum with solid elements,

167
00:09:15,918 --> 00:09:21,107
but that needs a nonlinear soil model, interface
elements, and a lot of calibration.

168
00:09:21,127 --> 00:09:25,013
For a pile-like conductor, industry
practice is more efficient.

169
00:09:25,632 --> 00:09:28,530
We replace the soil with a row
of independent,

170
00:09:28,550 --> 00:09:30,941
nonlinear springs along the conductor.

171
00:09:30,961 --> 00:09:33,225
This is called a Winkler foundation.

172
00:09:33,245 --> 00:09:36,397
Each spring resists lateral
movement at one depth,

173
00:09:36,417 --> 00:09:38,998
and its behaviour comes from a p-y curve:

174
00:09:39,018 --> 00:09:44,010
the soil resistance per metre, p, as a
function of the lateral displacement, y.

175
00:09:44,630 --> 00:09:47,459
For soft clay, the classic
curve is Matlock's.

176
00:09:47,479 --> 00:09:52,042
Resistance rises steeply at first, then
flattens out at an ultimate value.

177
00:09:52,062 --> 00:09:54,334
That ultimate value grows with depth,

178
00:09:54,354 --> 00:09:57,802
because deeper soil is stronger and
can't escape upward,

179
00:09:57,822 --> 00:09:59,970
so every spring gets its own curve.

180
00:09:59,990 --> 00:10:03,810
In the FE program, each curve is
entered as a table of points.

181
00:10:04,430 --> 00:10:09,371
To turn a curve into a spring, multiply the
resistance per metre by the length of

182
00:10:09,391 --> 00:10:11,208
conductor the node represents:

183
00:10:11,228 --> 00:10:14,393
half an element above it, and
half an element below.

184
00:10:14,413 --> 00:10:17,088
The vertical direction gets
its own springs:

185
00:10:17,108 --> 00:10:20,089
t-z curves for skin friction
along the conductor,

186
00:10:20,109 --> 00:10:22,723
and a Q-z curve for end
bearing at the tip.

187
00:10:23,344 --> 00:10:24,628
Now load the wellhead.

188
00:10:24,648 --> 00:10:29,368
The conductor bends, and the springs near the
mudline mobilise their resistance.

189
00:10:29,388 --> 00:10:32,331
The bending moment keeps growing
below the seabed,

190
00:10:32,351 --> 00:10:34,702
and peaks five metres below the mudline,

191
00:10:34,722 --> 00:10:37,724
nineteen percent higher than at
the mudline itself.

192
00:10:38,344 --> 00:10:43,309
Because the springs sit at the nodes, the element
length is also the spring spacing.

193
00:10:43,329 --> 00:10:46,573
A convergence study shows that
this model is forgiving:

194
00:10:46,593 --> 00:10:50,905
even four-metre elements are only about
four percent off at the wellhead.

195
00:10:50,925 --> 00:10:53,457
Use about half a diameter
near the mudline,

196
00:10:53,477 --> 00:10:57,789
where the soil reaction changes fastest,
and longer elements deeper down.

197
00:10:58,409 --> 00:11:02,131
A tempting shortcut is to fix the
conductor at the mudline.

198
00:11:02,151 --> 00:11:02,511
Don't.

199
00:11:02,531 --> 00:11:04,984
It deletes the flexibility of the soil.

200
00:11:05,004 --> 00:11:09,423
Here, it underestimates the wellhead
deflection by ninety-six percent,

201
00:11:09,443 --> 00:11:12,277
and the rotation by more than
eighty percent.

202
00:11:12,897 --> 00:11:15,926
Soil properties are uncertain,
so run lower bound,

203
00:11:15,946 --> 00:11:18,121
best estimate and upper bound soils.

204
00:11:18,141 --> 00:11:23,122
Softer soil pushes the peak moment deeper and
increases the wellhead deflection by

205
00:11:23,142 --> 00:11:23,426
half.

206
00:11:23,446 --> 00:11:26,780
Stiffer soil pulls the bending up
towards the wellhead.

207
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:30,805
For fatigue, use curves for cyclic
loading at small displacements,

208
00:11:30,825 --> 00:11:33,610
because that's where fatigue
damage builds up.

209
00:11:34,230 --> 00:11:35,903
And one trap in 3D models.

210
00:11:35,923 --> 00:11:38,443
Two independent springs, in x and in y,

211
00:11:38,463 --> 00:11:41,569
make the soil too strong in
diagonal directions:

212
00:11:41,589 --> 00:11:45,477
by twenty-six percent on the rising
part of Matlock's curve,

213
00:11:45,497 --> 00:11:48,212
and by up to forty-one percent at failure.

214
00:11:48,232 --> 00:11:52,510
Use a coupled radial spring, or line the
springs up with the load.

215
00:11:55,933 --> 00:11:56,696
Chapter four:

216
00:11:56,716 --> 00:11:58,984
boundary conditions at the interfaces.

217
00:11:59,004 --> 00:12:02,716
This is where the most important
modelling decisions are made,

218
00:12:02,736 --> 00:12:05,064
and our model has three key interfaces.

219
00:12:05,684 --> 00:12:09,091
The first is between the conductor
housing and the template.

220
00:12:09,111 --> 00:12:13,147
The housing stands in the well-slot
receptacle with a radial clearance,

221
00:12:13,167 --> 00:12:14,517
here thirty millimetres.

222
00:12:15,037 --> 00:12:17,797
Tying the housing to the
template would be wrong,

223
00:12:17,817 --> 00:12:19,952
and so would leaving them unconnected.

224
00:12:19,972 --> 00:12:21,824
The right model is a gap element.

225
00:12:21,844 --> 00:12:24,547
It carries no force while the
clearance is open,

226
00:12:24,567 --> 00:12:27,326
and the stiffness of the
template once it closes.

227
00:12:27,947 --> 00:12:31,802
That stiffness has to include the
template frame and its foundation.

228
00:12:31,822 --> 00:12:36,361
If we fix the suction anchors, the slot looks
almost eight times stiffer than it

229
00:12:36,381 --> 00:12:36,930
really is.

230
00:12:37,549 --> 00:12:38,861
Now increase the load.

231
00:12:38,881 --> 00:12:42,737
Up to about fifty kilonewtons, the
conductor carries everything.

232
00:12:42,757 --> 00:12:46,733
Then the gap closes, and the template
starts working as a fulcrum.

233
00:12:46,753 --> 00:12:51,153
At full load, the contact force is two
hundred and sixty-six kilonewtons,

234
00:12:51,173 --> 00:12:55,694
almost twice the riser load, and the bending
moment at the mudline drops by

235
00:12:55,714 --> 00:12:56,844
twenty-one percent.

236
00:12:57,464 --> 00:12:59,674
So the template can relieve the wellhead,

237
00:12:59,694 --> 00:13:03,644
but only if the template and its anchors
are designed to carry that load.

238
00:13:03,664 --> 00:13:06,037
Leave the gap out, and you
miss a load path.

239
00:13:06,658 --> 00:13:08,639
The contact geometry matters too.

240
00:13:08,659 --> 00:13:12,457
A single, short contact ring can only
transfer a lateral force.

241
00:13:12,477 --> 00:13:15,610
Two rings some distance apart can
transfer a moment.

242
00:13:15,630 --> 00:13:17,852
Put contacts where they really occur.

243
00:13:17,872 --> 00:13:21,429
And vertically, use compression-only
contact with friction,

244
00:13:21,449 --> 00:13:25,429
so the housing can bear on its landing
face, but also lift off it.

245
00:13:27,033 --> 00:13:31,710
The second interface is between the conductor
housing and the high-pressure housing.

246
00:13:31,730 --> 00:13:36,294
It controls how the bending moment passes from
the inner housing to the outer one.

247
00:13:36,914 --> 00:13:41,542
In the local solid model, every contact
surface becomes a surface-to-surface

248
00:13:41,562 --> 00:13:42,337
contact pair:

249
00:13:42,357 --> 00:13:47,045
the landing shoulder, the lock ring, and the
radial contacts above and below.

250
00:13:47,065 --> 00:13:51,753
Make the finer side the slave, and keep a
similar mesh density on both sides.

251
00:13:52,373 --> 00:13:57,391
Apply the lock ring preload in its own load
step, before the external loads.

252
00:13:57,411 --> 00:14:02,892
Here the preload is 7.3 MN, and the contact
pressure is even all the way around the

253
00:14:02,912 --> 00:14:03,488
shoulder.

254
00:14:04,108 --> 00:14:09,835
As the moment increases, the pressure builds on
the compression side and falls on

255
00:14:09,855 --> 00:14:11,041
the tension side.

256
00:14:11,061 --> 00:14:14,446
Above about 1.4 MNm, the
tension side lifts off.

257
00:14:14,466 --> 00:14:18,277
At 3 MNm, forty-three percent of
the shoulder is open.

258
00:14:18,896 --> 00:14:23,767
Lift-off changes the load path and roughly
halves the rotational stiffness of the

259
00:14:23,787 --> 00:14:24,129
joint.

260
00:14:24,149 --> 00:14:27,148
You can see the knee in the
moment-rotation curve.

261
00:14:27,168 --> 00:14:30,167
A tied, or bonded, contact would
miss all of that.

262
00:14:30,787 --> 00:14:34,014
The global beam model doesn't need
all of this detail.

263
00:14:34,034 --> 00:14:37,260
There, represent the interface
with nonlinear springs,

264
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:41,829
calibrated against the local model, so both
models share the same stiffness.

265
00:14:42,450 --> 00:14:46,350
The hottest spots in this model sit
around the lock ring groove,

266
00:14:46,370 --> 00:14:47,820
which has sharp corners.

267
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:50,392
Stress at a sharp corner is a singularity:

268
00:14:50,412 --> 00:14:52,107
refine, and it keeps rising.

269
00:14:52,127 --> 00:14:54,312
The model is fine for load transfer,

270
00:14:54,332 --> 00:14:58,537
but fatigue at the groove needs the real
radius and a converged mesh.

271
00:15:00,167 --> 00:15:04,906
The third interface, between the conductor and
the soil, we have already built:

272
00:15:04,926 --> 00:15:09,003
lateral p-y springs, axial t-z springs,
and a Q-z spring at the tip,

273
00:15:09,023 --> 00:15:10,871
all grounded at their far ends.

274
00:15:10,891 --> 00:15:15,329
Start them at the real mudline, and reduce
the soil strength near the top,

275
00:15:15,349 --> 00:15:17,920
where jetting or drilling
has disturbed it.

276
00:15:18,540 --> 00:15:22,894
Inside the conductor, the cement ties the
surface casing to the conductor.

277
00:15:22,914 --> 00:15:27,328
Model it with lateral links between the two
pipes, up to the top of cement.

278
00:15:27,348 --> 00:15:31,467
Above the cement, the pipes can only
touch, so use gap elements there.

279
00:15:32,087 --> 00:15:34,694
The welds are the simplest
interfaces of all.

280
00:15:34,714 --> 00:15:39,130
The conductor and its housing, and the casing
and the high-pressure housing,

281
00:15:39,150 --> 00:15:41,582
are continuous steel, so they share nodes.

282
00:15:42,201 --> 00:15:46,292
Finally, when you transfer loads from the
global model to the local one,

283
00:15:46,312 --> 00:15:48,748
apply them through a
distributing coupling.

284
00:15:48,768 --> 00:15:53,202
A rigid coupling holds the cut face flat, and
adds stiffness that isn't there.

285
00:15:53,222 --> 00:15:57,486
And place the cut a diameter or two away
from anything you want to measure,

286
00:15:57,506 --> 00:16:00,741
so the way the load is applied
doesn't affect the result.

287
00:16:04,167 --> 00:16:06,036
Before you believe any result, check it.

288
00:16:06,556 --> 00:16:08,975
Do the reactions balance the
applied loads?

289
00:16:08,995 --> 00:16:12,266
Does the deformed shape make sense
when you exaggerate it?

290
00:16:12,286 --> 00:16:14,536
Has the result you care about converged?

291
00:16:14,556 --> 00:16:17,373
Are the contacts open and closed
where you expect?

292
00:16:17,993 --> 00:16:19,834
Does a hand calculation agree?

293
00:16:19,854 --> 00:16:24,736
Here, Hetényi's solution for a beam on an
elastic foundation matches the linear

294
00:16:24,756 --> 00:16:28,459
spring version of our model, to
within a tenth of a percent.

295
00:16:29,079 --> 00:16:32,946
And how sensitive is the answer to the
things you don't know well,

296
00:16:32,966 --> 00:16:35,714
like soil strength, gaps,
preload and friction?

297
00:16:35,734 --> 00:16:40,191
If small changes move the result a lot,
report a range, not a single number.

298
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:42,161
So:

299
00:16:42,181 --> 00:16:46,554
choose the element by the physics, size the
mesh by the stress gradients,

300
00:16:46,574 --> 00:16:50,165
and set boundary conditions that
follow the real load paths.

301
00:16:50,985 --> 00:16:54,355
Do that, and your model will answer the
question you actually asked.

